A||N||21||Steve Normington||Blowing DIS||||1||
Z||000000||Steve Normington||07-11-2001||05:23 PM||||I think my 4 week old DIS ignition module may be bad (taking it to NAPA for testing Friday). Is there anything that could cause a ignition module to blow, ie bad ground, improper wiring to computer, etc? The coils were replaced at the same time as the module and I coated the module  with gobbs of dielectric.<P>------------------<BR>Once we have all the bugs ironed out we'll be running on flat bugs.||||reg||1
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||07-11-2001||06:07 PM||||what is it doing that is making you think it is blown? They can test them at most part stores. I thought mine was gone when I had my tech 4, it ended up being the sparkplug wires that were brand new! had too much resistence. Got some solid cores and it ran like new. <P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||07-11-2001||06:35 PM||||I'm not positive that it is bad, that's why I'm taking it to NAPA Friday. In case it is bad, I want to make sure whatever caused the problem doesn't blow the new module.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-11-2001||07:03 PM||||did you replace the crank sensor?||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||07-11-2001||10:53 PM||||Also, did you coat the backsides of the coils and the plate that the coils mount to with heat sink compound?  Using that is a must!  The dielectric in the terminals was also a very good idea.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steve Normington||07-12-2001||01:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>did you replace the crank sensor?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, the crank sensor puts out about 1 V when cranking (don't know the accuracy of the meter).<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>Also, did you coat the backsides of the coils and the plate that the coils mount to with heat sink compound?  Using that is a must!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is there a difference between the dielectric and heat sink compound? I used the dielectric as the heat sink compound between the module and the plate it mounts to. The engine was not run for more than a minute at a time, is that enough time to generate enough heat?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Joe Torma||07-12-2001||02:20 PM||||Are all the gounds intact on the engine and frame?  Long shot, but with my aftemarket DIS the manual warns about the engine using the DIS box as gound and shorting it out in the process.<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-12-2001||07:55 PM||||Do NOt coat the coil side of the module with anything. Use dielectric/heatsink grease only on the engine side. I also pack the terminals on the bottom of the coils with dielectric in addition to the crank sensor terminals.<P>The sheild plate on top of the module does 2 things. 1. it prevents EM pulses from the coils getting to the PIM. 2. it keeps the coils cooler.<P>Replace the crank sensor any time the PIM is going to be replaced. There isn't really a reliable way to test it. Passing that sort of test at idle doesn't mean it's good at higher RPM. Read in my cave about taking the brick off... two of the bolts must have sealer or you get an oil leak.<P>There are multiple grounds attached to the engine. ALL of them must be good or all sorts of odd things start happening.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Steve Normington||07-13-2001||11:42 PM||||Update: Took the DIS module to NAPA, it tested as defective. Got a new one, hooked it up. As soon as I tried to start it, the 5 amp ECM ignition fuse blew and it wouldn't start. Replaced fuse with new fuse, tried to restart and no start, no blown fuse. This is exactly what happened to the first module including the blowing of the fuse only once per module. I checked the polarity of the power and ground to the module and it is as the wiring diagram shows it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||07-14-2001||12:22 AM||||Acording to the book I have(haynes) ECM IGN fuse is a 10 amp job. According to my GM printouts... that fuse runs the ECM, ignition brick, and the TBI injector.<P>Look for open grounds, bad coils, bad plugs/wires, bad injector. The Module has 2 grounds. BOTH must be good for the system to work right. The main one is on the 2 wire connector. It's remotly posible the ECM has croaked.<P>The second is on the big connector. It's only purpose is to make sure the ECM and module are at the same level of ground.<P>These modules take tremendous abuse.  sounds like you have a dead short someplace to be doing this. Check every inch of the engine wire harness by hand.<P>rather than keep spending a fortune. go to a scrap yard and yank a couple complete bricks from Grand Am or Olds Cutlass Calais. (Olds had this motor thru 91.) You can use the ECM from these cars but you have to put a Fiero chip in them<P>S10 trucks also used these motors. You may be able to use one of those bricks. I can't remember for sure if they are identical modules. If the harness connectors are the same the the whole brick is. You can't use the truck ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Steve Normington||07-14-2001||11:52 AM||||Update to update: After some sleep, it seems that the fuse that was blowing was not the ECM fuse, but the TBI INJ 1 fuse. Seems like I have some weird wiring in there.<BR>theogre: Thanks for the suggestion about pulling modules from junkers. Any way to tell if the module is good or not?||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||07-14-2001||01:50 PM||||It's rather unusual to fry modules in this ignition. Just grab 2-3 of them. take the whole brick off. it's faster. If you take just the modules, save the shield plate that should be on top of them. <P>Save all the bolts and nuts... Some where along the line you'll very likely loose one and need the extras. Also, you can replace the F'ing bolt with the stud top with a pain one. It looks much cleaner without the unused stud sticking up.<P>Take someone big with you or a section of pipe. unfasten one end of the torque strut and pull the motor forward so you can get a little more room. (Pry off the tranny linkage to get it out of park.)<P>The fact you are blowing an inject 1 fuse is interesting.... Inj 1 and 2 circuits don't do anything on this engine. They are there for the V6. The only reason they have fuses in from the factory is to save having to mess with the fuse box on the assembly line. (To be 100% sure my drawings are right. I just physically tested it. The car runs perfectly without IJN 1 and 2.)<P>This pretty much gurantees a dead short. I'd pull the console and check by the ECM to make sure someone didn't hatchet it under there then work the harness from there back. It has to be melted or otherwise damaged some place.<P>That I can tell.... INJ 1 and 2 should dead end at C203 which I'm pretty sure is the large connector under the console laying below the ECM. I don't believe they pass back to the engine bay. (I don't have the 87 V6 wiring to see where they go. There are significant wire changes in 87. You can't trust Haynes or Chilton.)<P>If that's correct then the short could be inside the car. I'd look for damage to the harness under the console and dash as well.<P>Again, because I don't have 87V6 wiring, I'm not 100% sure but a few of the wires in question may go to the big block by the battery. That means you'll have to go over the entire harness in back.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||07-14-2001||03:45 PM||||I checked this while I was working on some ECM hacking this afternoon.<P>I have bad news for you.... The V6 Inj 1 and 2 circuits do dead end at C203 below the ECM. This means you definatly have a problem inside the car. Once you find that you still need to verify the wire in back to be sure it's all ok.<P>Just as bad.... you may have fried the ECM... read the other thread I just posted if you did.... ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Steve Normington||07-14-2001||07:11 PM||||Rereading my posts, I noticed that I forgot a tiny, almost insignifigant fact: the DIS, engine, ECM and ECM wiring are out of a 97 Olds Silhoutte 3.4 L and the Fiero is a 85 2.8 L (originally). The car has never been run with the Olds ECM and I am not positive about the condition of the ECM or that I wired the ECM to the car correctly. I be tracing the wiring tomorrow to look for shorts. Quick question, will running the engine with the ECM to DIS module connection removed harm either the ECM or DIS module? IE, can I run the engine with just the power connection and the crank position sensor connected?||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||07-14-2001||10:51 PM||||Just s little fact..... Ogre drops a boulder on you...<P>If the that module is like the ones I've worked with.....<P>It might start but you certainly can't drive it that way. The module can't adjust timing w/o the ECM. you'll be lucky if it even idles.<P>You've definatly botched the wiring someplace to have the problem discribed above. Just what is wrong only god knows at this point.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Joe Torma||07-15-2001||01:09 PM||||You have the 3.4L GEN III motor...and you are using the original PCM?  Are you using the automatic trans out of the van?  The stock PCM controls too much!!!  JUNK IT.  Try a 3.4/3.1 Beretta or GEN II harness/ECM.<P>If you used your stock trans, the Powetrain Control Module is not going to receive info from a half dozen sensors that you don't have....so it won't dive(run)right anyway.<BR>If you swapped the trans...you won't be able to go over 85 mph!!!<P>I have the same motor in my car...engineered the conversion myself.<P><BR>Joe<BR>3400 series 300hp<P>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-15-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Steve Normington||07-15-2001||11:03 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Just s little fact..... Ogre drops a boulder on you...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Your advice was still correct. I checked all the wiring and it is no longer blowing fuses or ignition modules. Don't know exactly what the problem was...<P>Thanks for the help.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>You have the 3.4L GEN III motor...and you are using the original PCM?  Are you using the automatic trans out of the van?  The stock PCM controls too much!!!  JUNK IT.  Try a 3.4/3.1 Beretta or GEN II harness/ECM.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do those ECM use the exact same ECM connections as the Gen III ECM's? I am NOT going to touch the wiring any more.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> What year Beretta should I look for?<P>PS. Are there custom ECM programs available since there is no PROM to reprogram?<p>[This message has been edited by Steve Normington (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Joe Torma||07-16-2001||07:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>  Do those ECM use the exact same ECM connections as the Gen III ECM's? I am NOT going to touch the wiring any more.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> What year Beretta should I look for?<P>PS. Are there custom ECM programs available since there is no PROM to reprogram?<P>[This message has been edited by Steve Normington (edited 07-15-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Unfortunately not.  Nobody will touch the OBD II computers.  I had to go with an expensive total engine control system...it is worth it, but the wiring is intense.  The Beretta harness is more like the 88 Fiero harness I'd look for an earlier 3.4 harness.<P><BR>Tell me about your setup!!!  I think we are the only people with the GEN III 3400 engine!<BR>What did you do about the front mount?  How about the alternator?!  Dogbone?<P>Joe<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Steve Normington||07-17-2001||12:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>Unfortunately not.  Nobody will touch the OBD II computers.  I had to go with an expensive total engine control system...it is worth it, but the wiring is intense.  The Beretta harness is more like the 88 Fiero harness I'd look for an earlier 3.4 harness.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know, part of the reason I started this is because it seemed to be a pretty rare thing. Of course, that means that I'm breaking ground for much of it. What computer system did you go with? I was looking at the Haltech E6K because that would work with the existing DIS and injectors.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>Tell me about your setup!!!  I think we are the only people with the GEN III 3400 engine!<BR>What did you do about the front mount?  How about the alternator?!  Dogbone?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If by the front mount you mean the engine mount by the harmonic balancer, I ground a bit off the oil pan and used the 85 Fiero mount. If you mean the little shock absorber, I'm using the two bolts that line up and some hope. I custom welded the alternator bracket (and the upper AC bracket) from scratch. I ground off a small section of the dogbone bracket and mounted it about 3/4" higher than stock. I'm using the center bolt hole on the exhaust side of the head (where the coil pack stud went) and one of the bolt holes in the side of the head. I took the dogbone crossover bracket, ground off the side that would hit the valve cover, welded a new stringer on the other side for strength, and welded a new plate for the new bolt pattern of the Al heads.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Joe Torma||07-17-2001||01:04 PM||||Interesting....do you have any pictures of the engine on the cradle?  I'll scan and post some of mine later.  I'd like to see what you did.  I fabricated a lot of parts... I made polyurethane mounts all the way around using Prothane GM transmission mounts(worked out awesome).  And I got rid of the stock dogbone because I used the stock high mount alternator...that I just upgraded to a 220amp.  <P><BR>Joe<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Steve Normington||07-18-2001||12:55 AM||||No pics yet, I'm waiting until its done. What are you using to stabilize the engine with the dogbone gone? Also, are you using the 24x crank sensor with the programable computer?||||reg||1
Z||000021||Joe Torma||07-18-2001||02:57 AM||||I made a polyurethane bushed "dog bone"...more like a stick    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ... to take the torque.  It bolts onto the side of the block and kinda props up the engine from the cradle.  May or may not be the best design...I guess I'll see.  It is threaded so I can restrict the engine's movement a [little] bit more if I want.<P>All the factory timing sensors are now gone, replaced by one trigger wheel on the front of the balancer.<P><BR>Joe<P>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-18-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
